Comparing the area within 250 m of a school to the rest of the city, on regular school days (excluding the pandemic closure periods) between 6 AM and 6 PM, both show the same steady decline (@fig-ped-by-year).


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Pedestrian collisions on regular school days between 6 AM and 6 PM, inside and outside 250 m of a school.](index_files/figure-html/fig-ped-by-year-1.png){#fig-ped-by-year fig-alt='Two bar charts by year, one for collisions within 250 m of a school and one for collisions beyond 250 m. Both decline from 2015 to 2022.' width=2400}
:::
:::


The pattern by traffic control also holds near schools: 15 of the 28 pedestrian collisions (54%) happened where some control was in place (@fig-traffic-control).


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![Pedestrian collisions within 250 m of a school, by traffic control at the location.](index_files/figure-html/fig-traffic-control-1.png){#fig-traffic-control fig-alt='Horizontal bar chart of the 28 pedestrian collisions near schools by traffic control type.' width=2400}
:::
:::


## Most collisions near schools involve no pedestrian or cyclist

@tbl-all-coll-near-sch counts every collision within 250 m of a school on a school day between 6 AM and 6 PM, with pedestrian and cyclist collisions broken out. The vast majority, about 95%, involved neither.


::: {#tbl-all-coll-near-sch .cell tbl-cap='Collisions of any kind within 250 m of a school on school days, 6 AM to 6 PM.'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

